Florence, a 2.7-mile wide asteroid, is expected to safely pass Earth on Sept. 1, from about 4.4 million miles away.
NASA says in a news release that Florence is the largest asteroid to pass our planet since the U.S. space agency started detecting and tracking "near-Earth" asteroids. The asteroid was measured by NASA's Spitzer Telescope and NEOWISE mission.
"While many known asteroids have passed by closer to Earth than Florence will on September 1, all of those were estimated to be smaller," Paul Chodas, manager of NASA's Center for Near-Earth Object Studies, said in the release.
