With an eye on keeping Fairhope a forward-thinking city, Councilwoman Diana Brewer has put a few discussion points on Monday's work session agenda, covering Uber, honeybees and egg-laying hens.
Uber is fast becoming a hot topic in the coastal region, with Mobile agreeing to allow the ride-sharing service to compete with traditional cabs and Orange Beach raising concerns about Uber drivers operating without business licenses.
Brewer said she's had good experiences using Uber in major cities and she wants to welcome the company to Fairhope.
Uber's marketing campaign for the Alabama Gulf Coast touts serving "Mobile, Gulf Shores, Orange Beach, or anywhere in between." But only Mobile has enacted an ordinance that lays out rules for Uber.
Responding to an AL.com inquiry, an Uber spokeswoman sent this statement: "Ridesharing is a new and innovative transportation option, and has a fundamentally different business model than taxis or shuttles. States, and cities like Mobile, have enacted sensible regulatory frameworks for ridesharing, and we welcome the opportunity to discuss a path forward with additional Alabama officials."
Brewer said she wasn't sure if Uber drivers were already ferrying Fairhope passengers. But she said the City Council should get out in front of the issue, even develop an ordinance because it would add a needed transportation option between Fairhope to Mobile.
"I think it would be a huge benefit to residents in Fairhope and vice-versa," she said. "The demand will prove whether or not that it's even needed. But I'd at least make it available if people do want to become an Uber driver. I think it could help. As difficult as it is to get back and forth across the bay, people might take advantage of it."
Regarding honeybees and chickens, a resident reached out to the city about having hives in his yard. Since the council is going to talk about bees, Brewer said it's a good time to also talk about welcoming egg-laying hens within the city limits.
According to Fairhope Planning and Zoning Director Jonathan Smith, the city zoning ordinance effectively bars bee-keeping and chicken-raising, relegating those to land that's in agricultural use.
