This is another Facebook story. My story. You won��t find me anywhere on Facebook these days, but you��ll find plenty of other Stephen Gordons to make up for it, because my name is common and my faces are many, each unique in their own way.
It wasn��t always like this. As is the case with many of my peers, I used to be a Facebook addict. The allure of reconnecting with former classmates, forgotten friends, and close or distant relatives piqued my curiosity, with probing hooks that sunk ever deeper into my defenseless psyche, provoking a chemical reaction that heightened my brooding self-awareness to new levels of sensitivity.
In other words, I had been reduced to the status of another real-time Facebook junkie: self-assured, slightly narcissistic, starved for attention and completely helpless, with a dire need for additional hooks to maintain the stream of dopamine surging across my brain.
This is a need that entailed knowing what my peers were up to, how age was treating them, where they lived and, most importantly, the manner in which they acknowledged my presence as they glanced at my profile. Would they leave gratifying comments, send lightweight invitations to rendezvous old stomping grounds, even contact me privately to catch up on old times? Would I be dazed from the spontaneous combustion of opportunity that threatened my established routine of calm retrospect? After several weeks, it sure seemed that way.
Then something snapped. Within my troubled core it all changed.
My need for an established identity within the digital landscape withered as my appetite for social networking weakened. The friends who shared my physical space were not the individuals I had come to befriend on Facebook. People I hadn��t spoken to in years were simply strangers who shared nothing in common, aside from an aftertaste that grew sweeter with nostalgia.
Even people I barely knew became Facebook friends, and though we never engaged in any type of correspondence, they served a useful purpose by padding my numbers to make me appear more popular than I actually was. Welcome to junior high.
I have no doubt that Facebook has served – and will continue to serve – a useful purpose for many individuals I know and respect. After all, where else can one learn so much about so many in such a short amount of time? It taps into a collective need to communicate rapidly and efficiently, to share of oneself on a level of openness that reaches epidemic proportions, to homogenize informal conversation to such a degree that things like what one had for dinner that night, who won the Lakers game, how cold it was in Vegas over Christmas, or why Coke tastes better than Pepsi takes on a deeper meaning.
In the midst off all this, Facebook is recording every keystroke of every user, amassing mountains of data both public and private, increasing in value with every word and every click, until it knows you better than the friends you thought you knew. It has become a melting pot of dreams, desires, thoughts, fears and idiosyncrasies, making you a star on a stage of your own making, in your own corner of the world.
Eventually, you will become another brick in the wall of supplicating text that may one day reach the heavens, but at least you will be on that wall, for what it��s worth.
When the day of online reckoning is finally here and each of us is accounted for, I have decided to not be grouped with the chosen ones of social networking. After the initial thrill of discovering that past and present could be joined together in a synaptic twist of fate, the intoxicating rush of chemicals began to wear off, exposing a hole that drained all of my original enthusiasm, reducing me to a caricature of my former self.
That my participation brightened the lives of those around me, that I was contributing to something magical and unprecedented on such a large scale, no longer held merit in the eyes of the beholder. Though I persuaded myself to keep an active profile in case somebody needed to reach me (whoever she was), it paled in comparison with my need to remove the hooks permanently.
Zombie-like, I trudged along, despising every second until I could no longer look at my online persona without feeling remorse and/or disgust.
This wasn��t me. The endless stream of status updates, the steady supply of photos from well-meaning individuals I no longer knew or cared about, the constant chatter of people renewing old ties in a very public setting, sometimes under scrutiny, the mindless investment of time and energy that was better spent someplace else.
In my convoluted thinking, I believe that the mystery of a lost person is at times more appealing than the actuality of knowing they are alive and well, their true value derived from scarcity. Reason enough to delete my Facebook account without hesitation.{g}
