Sharon Davis doesn't oppose the Slaters and the Green Knights dueling on the football field, but she thinks a more cooperative approach should be taken between the two in the arena of economic development.
In Pen Argyl and Bangor, "One Slate Belt" is the theme being floated by Davis, a coordinator with the Greater Lehigh Valley Chamber of Commerce's Borough Business Revitalization Program.
Through a set of beautification and revitalization projects under way in the two boroughs, Davis said, she hopes investors and future business developers will recognize the area's potential.
"They want to see that you're having a regional effect," Davis said. "It's all linked."
Bangor was the first of the two municipalities to buy into the program, Davis said. Money secured through grants, donations and volunteer efforts has gone toward facade restoration projects at the Bangor Trust Building and other residences and businesses around town.
Earlier this month, work began on an extensive mural downtown highlighting aspects of Bangor's history. That mural, along with the rest of the projects Davis is endorsing, come at no cost to the taxpayers, she noted.
By restoring and freshening aging infrastructure, the borough is unlocking the potential of what's already in place, Davis said.
"Our architecture tells the story of who we are," she said.
Bangor Mayor John Brown credited Davis and the revitalization program's work earlier this month when the mural was started, adding that "essentially it's another step in the revitalization of downtown."
Davis hopes the projects will help get a historic district designation for Bangor's downtown.
"There has been a void in recognizing that the Slate Belt needs some attention," Davis said.
Pen Argyl has also embraced the chamber's initiatives. Its latest project can be seen on borough sidewalks throughout the downtown area.
The Pen Argyl Wisdom Walk, as it's been coined, is a series of green-painted sidewalk steps that each carries some sort of inspirational quote. Davis calls it "healthy for the body and mind."
Quotes from historical figures such as Henry Ford, Martin Luther King Jr. and Winston Churchill are featured along the walk.
